<!-- LOCAL MODELS -->
<section class="lib-section" aria-labelledby="local-title">
  <div class="lib-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Local Models</div>
    <h2 id="local-title">Runs with any model, anywhere</h2>
    <p>mcp-librarian is model-agnostic. It speaks the MCP protocol over stdio -- it doesn't care what model is on the other side. Cloud or local, large or small.</p>
    <div class="lib-features-grid" role="list" style="margin-top: 24px;">
      <div class="lib-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Cloud Clients</h3>
        <p>Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf, and any MCP-compatible coding agent. Full feature support out of the box.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lib-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Local Models</h3>
        <p>Ollama, LM Studio, llama.cpp, or any local model behind an MCP-compatible client. Search, load, install, and browse work identically. Skill creation quality scales with model capability.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px; font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-3); line-height: 1.6;">All 14 tools work regardless of the model. The only quality-dependent feature is auto-research skill creation -- larger models produce better-structured skills from web sources. Searching, loading, installing packs, and browsing are model-independent operations handled entirely by the server.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECURITY -->
<section class="lib-section" aria-labelledby="security-title">
  <div class="lib-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Security</div>
    <h2 id="security-title">Defense in layers</h2>
    <p>mcp-librarian is designed for the threat model of an AI agent with file system access. Every layer assumes the agent may be manipulated through prompt injection in the knowledge base itself.</p>
    <ul class="lib-security-list" role="list">
      <li>
        <span class="lib-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
        <span class="lib-security-text"><strong>Ed25519 signing</strong> -- every skill is hashed and signed on write. The signature is verified on every load. A modified file fails the integrity check and is flagged before its content reaches the agent.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="lib-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
        <span class="lib-security-text"><strong>Content guard</strong> -- blocks prompt injection patterns in skill content. Legitimate instruction-like content inside fenced code blocks is permitted. Bare injections outside code blocks are rejected at write time.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="lib-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
        <span class="lib-security-text"><strong>Path guard</strong> -- all file operations are constrained to <code>MCP_LIBRARIAN_HOME</code>. Directory traversal attempts (<code>../</code>, absolute paths, symlink escapes) are rejected before any I/O occurs.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="lib-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
        <span class="lib-security-text"><strong>Minimal network surface</strong> -- no network access occurs except during explicit <code>install_pack</code> calls. The server makes no telemetry requests, no version checks, and no background connections of any kind.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
